#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079640 :tutorial/content "Using a calculator, we can evaluate the sine of the angle ![](images/17/17aa8e3ab57c18aa2d1a6d04653701085d3beec29c4805e9d6e18c22d586e466.svg) and get \n![](images/5c/5c5e85144e6e1aab226ee174adbb30a7edbe80bb9f8a5770d6205e81a4f6cc77.svg)\n\nBut what if we are given the value ![](images/6a/6a2e70809b6bc7130f042930e4e9a611ffafbf828c38d967ee5cf48d04d3ba72.svg) and we want to find the angle whose sine is this value? To do this, we can use the **inverse trigonometric functions**.\n\nIn the case of the sine, its inverse is **arcsine** (written as ![](images/19/1938ea6d9f1fa40ed0d039861e7c701e9ba3127c806c5a8eb7c1000b8ec5f941.svg) or ![](images/12/12f3b6602187e07917c7d4a7824afcd5a426c276a0065a780e6bc3bc84dd7411.svg)). If we apply arcsine to a sine value, it gives the corresponding angle:\n![](images/a5/a531b557f0d9668cbd2c5a55fdfcca2173e29faf4c7ed4a96bcc8ce76d8f23ce.svg)\n\nThe inverses of the other trigonometric functions work in the same way.\n\n- The inverse of sine is ![](images/39/39a3c758b61a3886b6945c0f29328934ad207c74ba713ef10770352ebbe69f32.svg)\n\n- The inverse of cosine is ![](images/9b/9b8a569b724129728610b4bd7ba28966ea13c064d08000075312f294724f4e45.svg)\n\n- The inverse of tangent is ![](images/a1/a111203310c10fdfa70deb7b5e7a2b1f6658310e4d13140ad84369beeaf46b19.svg)\n\nTry it for yourself. But make sure that your calculator is in \"degrees\" mode!\n\n**Note:** In this topic, we only deal with acute angles. Things get a little more complicated for non-acute angles, but you'll learn about that later!" :tutorial/id #uuid "259189af-6378-518d-a123-d831688aed97" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2450 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079641 :tutorial/content "The inverse trigonometric functions are particularly useful when we need to find the measure of an angle in a right triangle.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the measure of the angle $\\theta$ indicated in the triangle below.\n\n \n\n![](images/85/85c384f5cbfc9d9bfa4ffebeafc3dbe66eb756e71f4790b0d9566ebdab559f9c.png)\n\nSince we are given the length of the hypotenuse and opposite sides to $\\theta,$ we can use sine to find the angle measurement. We have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\sin (\\theta) =  \\dfrac{\\textrm{opposite}}{\\textrm{hypotenuse}}  &= \\dfrac{5}{7}.\n\t}\n$$\n\nWe now calculate $\\theta$ using the inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\sin (\\theta)   &= \\dfrac{5}{7}\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\arcsin( \\sin (\\theta)) &= \\arcsin\\left(\\dfrac{5}{7} \\right)\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\theta &= \\arcsin\\left(\\dfrac{5}{7} \\right)\\[5pt]\n&=(45.584\\,6...)^\\circ \\[5pt]\n\t\t&\\approx 46^\\circ\n\t}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." :tutorial/id #uuid "8a55899c-f08a-5577-a1de-9fb483947ddf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2451 :tutorial/title "Finding the Measure of an Angle in a Right Triangle Using Trigonometry"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079648 :tutorial/content "If we have a trigonometric equation like\n\n$$\n\\cos \\theta = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nand we want to solve for $\\theta,$ we can apply the corresponding inverse trigonometric function to both sides of the equation to \"cancel\" out the trigonometric function.\n\nHere, the trigonometric function is $\\cos,$ so the inverse trigonometric function is $\\arccos,$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ \\theta  & ={60}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9953e2ac-f857-5760-a8b2-c5c56367a048" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2560 :tutorial/title "Applying an Inverse Trigonometric Function to Both Sides of an Equation"}]}
